===Etymology 1===
Earlier ''mus'', ''moos'', from an {{der|en|alg-eas}} language name for the animal, such as {{der|en|wam|moos|moos, mws}}, {{cog|xnt|moos}} or {{cog|aaq|mos}} (cognate to {{cog|abe|moz}}), from {{der|en|alg-pro|*mo·swa||it strips}}, referring to how a moose strips tree bark when feeding: compare {{cog|wam|moos-u||he strips, cuts smooth}}.<ref>{{R:Etymonline}}</ref><ref>{{R:Dictionary.com}}</ref>

====Noun====
{{en-noun|moose|s|pl2qual=dated, rare|meese|pl3qual=nonstandard, jocular}}

# The largest member of the [[deer]] family ({{taxlink|Alces americanus|species}}, sometimes included in {{taxfmt|Alces alces|species}}), of which the male has very large, [[palmate]] [[antler]]s.
#: {{ux|en|We saw a '''moose''' at the edge of the woods.}}
# Any of the extinct moose-like deer of the genera ''Cervalces'' and ''Libralces''.
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=2018|author=w:Tim Flannery|title=Europe: A Natural History|page=152|passage=Europe&rsquo;s giant beavers lived at the same time as the first '''moose''', ''Libralces gallicus''.}}
# {{lb|en|figuratively|derogatory|colloquial}} An [[ugly]] person.

=====Usage notes=====
* The usual plural of {{m-self|en|moose}} is {{m-self|en|moose}}; compare the names of many animals, such as {{m|en|sheep}}, {{m|en|deer}} and {{m|en|fish}}, which are also invariant.
* Other plurals are rare and non-standard: {{m|en|mooses}} (with the usual English plural-forming suffix {{m|en|-s}}) and {{m|en|meese}} (jocularly formed by analogy to {{m|en|goose}} → {{m|en|geese}}).

